				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"13": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "PATTERNS\n\nPatterns are a (ever expanding) category of nodes that produce values in given patterns.\nObvious choices here are nodes like the Circle Pattern and Grid Pattern nodes.\nThese nodes produce a collection of coordinates that you can use to move things around.\n\nAssignment: Use the Grid Pattern node in the example below and adjust the parameters until you have a grid of 21 rectangles.\n\nThe Linear node is also a node from the pattern category.\nThis node takes two colors and produces a collection of colors (when set to color mode), spread out over a gradient, equal to its size.\n\nAssignment: Set the size of the Linear  node to less than 10. Can you spot the repetition that Wire creates due to different collection sizes?\n\nWe encourage you to explore the entire category of pattern nodes, but there is one in particular that you should know about: the Linear node.\nThe Linear node produces a collection of float values that are linearly distributed between its min and max values. In the node panel you can select what float type you would like to produce and whether or not you want to include the max. This node is often used to offset phases, distribute visuals over an axis or make incremental changes to scale."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 1803.04638671875,
					"width": 1054.3348388671875,
					"x": 1201.0751953125,
					"y": 676.5
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ff02bbff",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"14": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "JOINING\n\nThe Join node can be used to combine multiple collections or single signals into a collection.\nIn this example we are joining 3 shapes into a collection.\nThis prevents us from creating 3 separate Edge and Transform nodes.\n\nAssignment: delete the shapes and the Join node. Create three different shapes and Join them together.\n\nPro Tip: select multiple nodes with the same type and use the shortcut CMD\/CTRL + J  to quickly join them together."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 555,
					"width": 1054,
					"x": 82.5002288818359375,
					"y": 1302.971923828125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "fff26eb5",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"15": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SPLITTING\n\nSplitting is the opposite of joining in that it will take a collection and output each element from that collection as a single signal.\n\nIn this example we are using the Split node to break up the coordinates of the Grid Pattern node.\nNext, we use a Join node to select which elements we want to use.\n\nAssignment: disconnect the Split and Join nodes and rearrange it to your liking."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 555,
					"width": 1054,
					"x": 83.45556640625,
					"y": 1923.6220703125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffae7bff",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"16": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXPANDING\n\nThe Expand node allows you to grow a collection to a larger size.\nIn the node panel you can select what wrapping mode to use.\n\nIn this example we've used the Expand node to grow a collection of two colors into a collection of 12 colors using the Ping Pong wrapping mode.\n\nAssignment: Try different wrapping modes, Linear sizes and Expand sizes. This is a great way to learn about how Wire updates to the largest collection size."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 595.1439208984375,
					"width": 1054,
					"x": 82.5002288818359375,
					"y": 2563.58642578125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ff20c7bb",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"17": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "MERGING\n\nMerging is the opposite of expanding and allows you to merge a collection down to a single channel.\nDo note that there are other nodes like Converge and Concatenate that fulfill similar tasks for other data types.\n\nIn this example we use Merge to create one big shape that we can then take the outline of.\n\nAssignment: Remove the Merge node to see the difference between the merged and non-merged result. "
					}
				},
